{
  "dom_id": "step-q40401",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}$, where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 57\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+57\\cdot 7}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+399}{1+49} \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{50} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$",
